WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.610
Remember that show "Boy Meets World"?

2
00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:03.460
Did you guys watch that?
Of course.

3
00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:04.460
Of course.

4
00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.250
You might remember Mr. Feeny, his teacher.

5
00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.510
I loved Mr. Feeny.

6
00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.420
Well, his real name, the actor is William Daniels,

7
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.249
and he just turned 95 years old.

8
00:00:15.249 --> 00:00:19.610
(audience cheering)
Happy birthday, Mr. Feeny.

9
00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:21.800
And "People Magazine" talked to William

10
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.470
about his long career and his successful marriage

11
00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.650
of 70 years.
Oh!

12
00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:29.380
I know, to actress, Bonnie Bartlett, look at them.

13
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.840 line:15%
They met back in college and they've shared a life together

14
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.810 line:15%
acting and living as partners.

15
00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:39.240 line:15%
And William says their secret is adapting.

16
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.610 line:15%
He says, "When you hurt each other,

17
00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:44.490 line:15%
the key to recover, grow, change, and adapt."

18
00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.690 line:15%
And what I loved about this answer is

19
00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.160 line:15%
it wasn't just sugary, right?

20
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.710
It wasn't like laugh or never go to bed angry.

21
00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.690
It was about like we change,

22
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.350
and if you love somebody now how they are,

23
00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:58.550
they're gonna change as you live 70 years together.

24
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.330
So can you love them as they grow?

25
00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.720
You have to adapt to them.

26
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:02.960
I thought that was so insightful.

27
00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.001
I love this.
Mr. Feeny teaching us again.

28
00:01:05.001 --> 00:01:06.743
(audience clapping)

29
00:01:06.743 --> 00:01:09.530
Thank you, thank you for that advice.

30
00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.890
Well, we've gotta take a moment to celebrate

31
00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.497
the life and work of an incredible woman.

32
00:01:14.497 --> 00:01:17.630
"CNN" reports, Betty Reid Soskin,

33
00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:20.480
the oldest ranger in the National Park Service,

34
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.033
well, she just retired, Rossy, at the age of 100.

35
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.260 line:15%
Wow!
That's right.

36
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:30.490 line:15%
Betty worked at a park in Richmond, California,

37
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.760 line:15%
that is dedicated to highlighting the works

38
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.270 line:15%
of Americans on the home front during World War II,

39
00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.350 line:15%
and Betty was one of those women.

40
00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:40.470 line:15%
After facing discrimination in the Air Force,

41
00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.830 line:15%
she went on to work for the Boilermakers auxiliary union.

42
00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.030 line:15%
Yeah, and Betty's work shed light

43
00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:48.360 line:15%
on the diverse group of Americans who supported the war,

44
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.760 line:15%
but often went unnoticed in the history books.

45
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.230
And you can hear Betty's entire story

46
00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.190 line:15%
in her own words online,

47
00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:56.840 line:15%
and the info's on our website.

48
00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.000 line:15%
Congratulations, Betty, and thank you

49
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.230 line:15%
for your incredible service, 100 years.

50
00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:05.290
Like that is an extraordinary life.

